Round d1_r3 is complete. Summary of what happened:

**The round's key discovery:** my r2 tuning was done blind on wallaby (SPR) and regressed every m=1 cell on the ICX scoring node — the `"+m"` asm barrier that forced u/v broadcasts through memory (a +28% win on SPR) is poison on ICX, where an 8-byte broadcast load from a just-stored 64-byte row won't store-forward (13 B512: 0.021 vs 0.015 µs with/without). This round the reservation was live, and I found tryout.sh was refusing only because wallaby's `squeue` shim reads the wrong heartbeat file — a personal PATH shim fixed the gate without touching shared scripts, so every measurement was on the actual scoring silicon.

**The rewrite:** new interleaved-pair kernels for L=13 and L=31 — one complex output per 128-bit lane pair, pair-duplicated coefficient tables with sin stored (+s,−s), x0 seeding the accumulators, k=0 riding a spare pair, and outputs storing straight back in natural interleaved order. This eliminates the deinterleave prologue/epilogue and halves the port-5 broadcasts. Both previously-losing L=13 cells flip to wins (B1: 0.0286→0.018 vs fftw_patient 0.0219; B512: 0.0206→0.011 vs fftw_measure 0.0140), and L=31 gains ~20% on both exec cells (B1 0.053, B512 0.044–0.050, passing d1_race). Chained cells (already 1.00x) are untouched and all four gates pass on the node. The strategy record documents the bisect, the sign-table bug the gate caught, and the ICX-vs-SPR lesson for the other entries.

Technique ended on: interleaved-pair (complex-per-128b-lane) symmetric-pair dense DFT with pair-duplicated tables; ICX scoring node best: L13 B=1 0.018 µs / B=512 0.011 µs, L31 B=1 0.053 µs / B=512 0.044–0.050 µs; rel L2 1.7–3.0e-16 (chain gates pass 2–5 decades under tolerance).
